<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crown of Thorns</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Holy Crown</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crown of Thorns is a relic venerated as the crown placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s head before the crucifixion. It was kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> until Emperor Baldwin II of the Latin Empire, desperate for money, pawned it to Venetian merchants; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> acquired it in 1238 and built the Sainte-Chapelle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to house it. It survived the 2019 fire at Notre-Dame de Paris, where it was kept.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crown-of-thorns.jpg" alt="Crown of Thorns" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: El Greco · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_Carrying_the_Cross_1580.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
